{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# J9 — Make the DC transport actually drive a writing reconcile pass\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nStory J7 [rebar:b4e3-1de0-3188-40a1] landed the DC backend's port conformance and made it reachable\nthrough config, then drove it against a REAL Jira Data Center instance for the first time (GitHub\nActions run 30604756524). The writing path CRASHES:\n\n    ERROR: reconcile_once raised: 'JiraDataCenterTransport' object has no attribute\n    'set_entity_property'\n\n`--mode dry-run` passes; `--mode bootstrap-strict` exits 1. So epic e369's headline capability —\nJira Server/Data Center support — does not work yet, and the epic cannot honestly close without this.\n\nFiled as [rebar:a357-b747-ece9-4cf5]; this story is its implementation.\n\n## The root cause is a PORT defect, and the gap is TWELVE members\n\nAn AST audit over `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/*.py` — collecting every method invoked on a\n`client`/`transport` receiver, diffed against `JiraDataCenterTransport`'s methods and against every\nProtocol in `_backend.py` — measured the real gap:\n\n**Called by the core, ABSENT from the DC transport (12):**\n`delete_issue`, `delete_issue_link`, `get_comments`, `get_issue_by_rest`, `get_issue_links`,\n`get_parent_map`, `remove_label`, `set_entity_property`, `set_issue_property`, `set_parent`,\n`set_reporter`, `validate_assignee_exists`.\n\n**Declared on NO Protocol in `_backend.py`: the same 12.**\n\n**The dominant failure mode is SILENT DEGRADATION, not crashing** — which inverts the obvious framing\nof this story. Classifying every call site by whether an enclosing `try` swallows `Exception`:\n\n| behaviour | count | members |\n|---|---|---|\n| propagates (crashes) | 2 | `get_issue_by_rest`, `set_reporter` |\n| swallowed at EVERY site | 7 | `delete_issue_link`, `get_comments`, `get_issue_links`, `set_issue_property`, `set_parent`, `validate_assignee_exists`, `get_parent_map` (the last via `hasattr`, `fetcher.py:448`) |\n| MIXED — crashes at some sites, swallowed at others | 3 | `delete_issue`, `remove_label`, `set_entity_property` |\n\nSo a DC deployment does not mostly crash: it mostly **appears to converge while quietly not syncing\ncomments, links, parents, issue properties, or validating assignees.** `set_entity_property` is\nswallowed at 2 of its 3 sites and crashes at the third — which is the ONLY reason any of this was\ndiscovered. The crash was the lucky case.\n\nThis has a direct consequence for the acceptance bar: \"no `AttributeError`\" is the WRONG criterion,\nbecause it is already true at most sites and true for the wrong reason. Fixing only the crash would\nproduce a green live run with the silent gaps fully intact — a worse outcome than today, because the\ngreen run would be taken as proof the capability works.\n\nA METHODOLOGICAL NOTE, because it changed the answer twice: an audit that matches only\n`client.method(...)` CALL forms under-counts, because the reconciler frequently passes the method as\na VALUE — `_call_with_retry(client.delete_issue_link, link_id)` (`dispatch_one.py:731`),\n`_call_with_retry(client.set_entity_property, ...)` (`dispatch_one.py:321`). The audit must collect\nevery ATTRIBUTE ACCESS on a `client`/`transport` receiver. A first pass using the narrow pattern\nreported ten and missed `delete_issue_link` and `set_parent`; the corrected pass reports twelve.\n\nAll twelve ARE implemented for Cloud, spread across `adapters/jira/acli_rest.py`, `acli.py` and\n`acli_graph.py`.\n\nSo `set_entity_property` is not \"the\" missing method — it is the FIRST one a live writing pass\nhappened to reach. Eleven more sit behind it. **Implementing only `set_entity_property` would move the\ncrash one call deeper** and produce another red run, looking like progress while the capability stays\nbroken.\n\n`TicketTransport` declares six members (`create_issue`, `get_issue`, `update_issue`,\n`transition_issue_by_name`, `add_label`, `search_issues`); the core reaches for twenty-one distinct\ntransport members. The port describes under a third of its own requirement. That is exactly why `isinstance` conformance, the backend contract suite,\nand 1636 reconciler unit tests were all green against a transport that cannot complete a writing pass.\n**Conformance to an incomplete port proves less than it appears to** — the same class as J6's\n`add_label` defect, where the only oracle was a fake mirroring the author's assumption.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. Implement the missing transport methods on `JiraDataCenterTransport` against DC's REST v2 API,\n   reading each Cloud counterpart first rather than inventing a shape. `set_entity_property` is\n   Cloud's alias for `set_issue_property`, which PUTs to\n   `/rest/api/3/issue/{key}/properties/{prop}` with the value sent **verbatim as the body** — an\n   earlier Cloud bug (0b27) wrapped it in `{\"value\": ...}` and silently stored the wrong shape, so\n   copy the corrected contract, not the intuition. DC is REST **v2**.\n2. Where a method is genuinely not applicable to DC, it must raise a clear `NotImplementedError`\n   naming the DC limitation — never be left absent to fail as `AttributeError` mid-pass. Each such\n   decision needs evidence, not assumption.\n3. Make absence detectable AT CONSTRUCTION rather than at some call site months later. `TicketTransport`\n   is currently `class TicketTransport(Protocol)` with **no `@runtime_checkable`**, so\n   `isinstance(x, TicketTransport)` raises `TypeError` rather than returning `False` — verified\n   directly, and it means the naive \"just isinstance it\" mechanism does not work. So: add\n   `@runtime_checkable` to `TicketTransport` (matching `Backend` and the capability protocols, which\n   already carry it), declare the required members on it, and assert conformance where a backend is\n   BUILT — `_build_jira_datacenter_backend` in `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py` and the Cloud\n   equivalent — so a missing member fails loudly at construction.\n4. Add a STRUCTURAL TEST that every transport member the core reaches for is declared on the port.\n   It must collect EVERY attribute access on a `client`/`transport` receiver, not just call forms:\n   a call-form-only audit silently under-counts (see the methodological note below) and would let the\n   thirteenth member slip in unnoticed. This is what stops the class of defect recurring on the next\n   backend.\n\n**Alternative rejected:** implement `set_entity_property` alone. It fixes one crash, leaves eleven,\nand leaves the port still misdescribing what the core needs.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py` — implement (or\n  explicitly `NotImplementedError`) the ELEVEN unguarded absentees listed in the audit above, plus a\n  deliberate decision on `get_parent_map`. **NOT just `set_entity_property`** — implementing one\n  method moves the crash one call deeper and produces another red live run.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_backend.py` — add `@runtime_checkable` to `TicketTransport`\n  (it currently lacks it, so `isinstance` against it raises `TypeError`) and declare the members the\n  core actually requires.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py` and\n  `adapters/jira/backend.py` — assert transport conformance in the FACTORY, where the backend is\n  built, so a missing member fails loudly at construction rather than at a call site later.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/backend_support.py` — `FakeTransport` gains whatever members the\n  widened port declares, since it is the contract suite's stand-in and must satisfy it.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/` — a structural test for the audit: every transport attribute the\n  core reaches for is declared on the Protocol.\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_reconcile_pass.py` — already written by J7; it is the oracle and\n  must go from FAIL to PASS unmodified. **Do not weaken it to accommodate the fix.**\n\n## Testing\n\n- unit: each implemented method issues the right REST call, asserted against a fake client. For\n  `set_entity_property` this includes\n  that the property KEY and VALUE reach the endpoint intact, since a silently wrong shape breaks\n  correlation without raising.\n- structural: the core-call-sites-vs-Protocol audit is a TEST, not a one-off grep, so a future core\n  module reaching for an undeclared transport method fails CI. Give it teeth with a synthetic case,\n  mirroring `test_jira_family_boundary_heldout.py::test_checker_flags_a_synthetic_violation`.\n- live: J7's `test_reconcile_pass.py` must pass in the `jira-dc-harness` CI job.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] ALL TWELVE absent members are addressed — each either implemented on\n      `JiraDataCenterTransport` or raising `NotImplementedError` naming the DC limitation, never\n      simply absent: `delete_issue`, `delete_issue_link`, `get_comments`, `get_issue_by_rest`,\n      `get_issue_links`, `get_parent_map`, `remove_label`, `set_entity_property`,\n      `set_issue_property`, `set_parent`, `set_reporter`, `validate_assignee_exists`\n- [ ] the acceptance bar is NOT \"no `AttributeError`\". For each of the SEVEN members whose call sites\n      swallow `Exception` at every site, and the THREE mixed ones, a test asserts the member is\n      actually PRESENT on the transport — because absence at those sites produces silent degradation,\n      so \"the pass converged\" and \"no exception escaped\" are both true today while nothing works\n- [ ] a test asserts a DC transport with a member REMOVED is rejected at construction (not at a later\n      call site), proving the guard fires\n- [ ] `set_entity_property` specifically is implemented and unit-tested, with the test asserting the\n      property key and value reach the REST layer INTACT — Cloud bug 0b27 stored `{\"value\": x}`\n      instead of `x`, and a wrong shape breaks correlation WITHOUT raising, so \"it did not error\" is\n      not evidence here\n- [ ] `TicketTransport` is made `@runtime_checkable` and declares the members the core requires —\n      without that decorator `isinstance(x, TicketTransport)` raises `TypeError` rather than\n      returning `False` (verified), so the construction-time guard cannot work at all as a bare\n      isinstance check\n- [ ] the conformance assertion runs where a backend is BUILT (`_build_jira_datacenter_backend` and\n      the Cloud equivalent), so a missing member is a loud construction failure\n- [ ] a structural test asserts every transport member the core reaches for is declared on the port,\n      collecting EVERY attribute access on a `client`/`transport` receiver — not just `client.x(...)`\n      call forms, since a call-form-only audit silently under-counts (it missed `delete_issue_link`\n      and `set_parent` here). Proven to FIRE on a synthetic undeclared member\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a full writing reconcile pass (`bootstrap-strict`) against the J5 harness converges with no\n      unhandled exception — J7's `test_dc_reconcile_pass_raises_no_unhandled_exception` passes for a\n      WRITING mode, not only dry-run\n- [ ] [operator-attested] an immediately repeated writing pass writes nothing (idempotence) —\n      J7's `test_a_repeated_dc_reconcile_pass_writes_nothing` passes\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a DC-created ticket carries `creation_channel == 'jira'` — J7's\n      `test_a_dc_created_ticket_carries_the_shared_jira_provenance` passes\n- [ ] the three live tests above pass WITHOUT any edit to `test_reconcile_pass.py` (`git diff` shows\n      that file unchanged) — the oracle is not adjusted to fit the fix\n\n## Note on the moved criteria\n\nThe three `[operator-attested]` live criteria were J7's. They are relocated here, not dropped: J7\ncannot satisfy them (its own scope is landed and verified), and this is the story whose fix makes them\npassable. Epic e369 therefore still requires a proven live writing pass before it closes."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785477547289800001,"uuid":"782de258-c8f0-4f7c-8ceb-8c9334d3f48e"}